---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="SQL workspace, steps, and migrations — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">For Developers</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">SQL workspace, steps, and migrations</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Environment gate</h2>
            <p>
                Interactive SQL and schema refresh for this role always resolve the
                <strong class="text-foreground">Development</strong> endpoint. The UI shows a
                Development badge, not an environment picker. Posting
                <code class="text-foreground">environmentKind=production</code> is rejected
                in the controller and again in the SQL service before target resolution.
                Operators (CanOperate) may switch Staging or Production; a non-Development
                schema refresh overwrites the one snapshot per connection.
            </p>

            <h2>Monaco and the schema cache</h2>
            <p>
                The editor is vendored locally. Dialect follows the logical connection's
                provider: PostgreSQL, SQL Server, or MySQL. Completion uses a fetched,
                normalized catalog — schemas, tables, views, columns, keys, indexes,
                routines — plus pipeline parameter names. Pipeline execution must not
                depend on the cache being current. Parser or model “validation” is never
                presented as a guarantee that production SQL is correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                The standalone workspace supports bounded preview with controlled
                parameters, a result grid, explain-plan retrieval where the provider
                supports it, and read-only diffs between versions. Bounded preview is
                not a production run.
            </p>

            <h2>SqlAssist</h2>
            <p>
                Assistance proposes SQL against a <strong class="text-foreground">schema source</strong>
                (a design-time catalog) and the pipeline contract. It never auto-publishes.
                A human reviews, edits, and publishes. Embeddings and chat history live
                on the control database (PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector). For artifact sinks,
                assistance must emit logical tokens such as
                <code class="text-foreground">{"{{ stage.name }}"}</code> and
                <code class="text-foreground">{"{{ input.name }}"}</code>, never physical
                temp table names or secrets. Dedicated walkthrough:
                <a href="/docs/developer-sql-assist" class="text-link hover:underline">SQL Assist</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Step handlers</h2>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                    <thead><tr class="border-b border-border text-left"><th class="py-2 pr-4">Handler</th><th class="py-2">Role</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">sql_command / sql_query / stored_procedure / validation_query / database-sql</td><td class="py-2">Dialect SQL on the bound target. Default unsafe for auto-retry.</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">database-query-to-parquet</td><td class="py-2">Capture a result set as Parquet. Default safe for auto-retry.</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">external-parquet</td><td class="py-2">Structural root seeded from an uploaded file at trigger. No SQL, no connection. Default safe. Not schedulable. <a href="/docs/developer-external-parquet" class="text-link hover:underline">External Parquet ingest</a>.</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">artifact-sql</td><td class="py-2">DuckDB SQL over Parquet inputs on an eligible agent. Default safe.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">staged-database-sql</td><td class="py-2">The only database sink. Bulk-load, then your merge SQL. Default unsafe for auto-retry. No ArtifactToTable.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Do not use Dapper for row-by-row bulk loading. Sinks use provider bulk
                APIs (COPY, SqlBulkCopy, MySqlBulkCopy) then developer SQL on the same
                connection/transaction. You never see the physical staging table name;
                tokens are substituted.
            </p>
            <p>
                Multi-input Artifact SQL and Staged Database SQL are data joins: they
                stay <strong class="text-foreground">Wait all</strong>. Publish rejects
                Wait any on those handlers and on any step whose config binds
                <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code>. Classic SQL (and a
                Parquet extract with no <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code>)
                may use Wait any when two or more inbound edges are control gates —
                <a href="/docs/developer-join-mode" class="text-link hover:underline">Wait all and Wait any</a>.
                Handler defaults for in-run auto-retry (writes unsafe, extracts and
                Artifact SQL safe):
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Step retry policies</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Migrations</h2>
            <p>
                Ordered, coded SQL files. After apply, the <strong class="text-foreground">target</strong>
                records what ran. Restoring a database from backup does not confuse
                Querial: history on that database is the truth. Apply policy can be
                Manual, OnDeploy, or BeforeRun. New publishes require every step and
                migration to be bound to a logical connection.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
